minors.issue:
Several common dense valid shapes fit well below the documented 'typical records 70 to 84%', but the fit list does not mention them. Measured through Factory.Build and Handle at the 10 MiB default, largest valid body that passes:
- (spec) an array of decimals under {"type":"number","minimum":-1000,"maximum":1000}: 60.4%, working-set limit (decision charge 13.2x the body), against 80.7% with type number alone;
- (spec) [timestamp, value] pairs under prefixItems integer/number: 45.8%, working-set limit;
- (bounds) arrays of [lon, lat] pairs under {"type":"array","minItems":2,"maxItems":2,"items":{"type":"number","minimum":-180,"maximum":180}}: 35.1% (3,683,471 B), working-set limit (decide 83.8 MB, about 416 bytes per 18-byte pair, peak heap 30.7 MB);
- (bounds) time series {"points":[{"t":int,"v":number},...]} with required and additionalProperties false: 48.1% (5,040,276 B), reservation limit;
- (spec) uniqueItems array of integer ids: 33.1%, and of short string tags: 62.1%, both working-set limit (duplicates map charged 192 bytes per item).
The charges are sound (the library's arrays never stop early in boolean mode) and within the accepted R-77 envelope; only the documentation is incomplete, so operators cannot see when to raise maxRequestBodyBytes. Typical records otherwise fit as R-77 states (OpenAPI-style nullable users 99.9%, 64-byte-key maps 99%, allOf inheritance 81%, JSON:API 67%). Probes: zz_r6spec_test.go TestR6MapFit and TestR6MoreFit (bisected to 1%).
